1
00:00:00,463 --> 00:00:03,259
Ask “Why does zero pass validation?”

2
00:00:03,342 --> 00:00:04,865
in a fresh chat.

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:09,090
Depending on the session, Copilot may
already receive parts of the active

4
00:00:09,145 --> 00:00:11,180
editor or search the workspace.

5
00:00:11,925 --> 00:00:14,749
Look for the sources cited in its
answer.

6
00:00:14,832 --> 00:00:18,736
Even a correct guess is easier to
evaluate once you know where it came

7
00:00:18,791 --> 00:00:19,677
from.

8
00:00:19,788 --> 00:00:22,161
Next we will choose the evidence
deliberately.

9
00:00:24,486 --> 00:00:27,421
Select three things from the training
checkout.

10
00:00:27,476 --> 00:00:29,719
The issue tells you what should happen.

11
00:00:29,774 --> 00:00:32,553
The validator tells you what the program
does now.

12
00:00:33,208 --> 00:00:36,724
The test file tells you what behavior is
already covered.

13
00:00:36,807 --> 00:00:40,102
If you only have the issue, you can
explain the rule.

14
00:00:40,157 --> 00:00:44,431
With the source, you can identify
exactly which comparison breaks it.

15
00:00:46,435 --> 00:00:50,311
Open the validator and the test file in
VS Code.

16
00:00:50,394 --> 00:00:53,800
Use the chat attachment control to add
those files.

17
00:00:53,883 --> 00:00:58,026
Include the synthetic issue text and
inspect the list before sending.

18
00:00:58,714 --> 00:01:02,563
Confirm the paths are from the checkout
you intend to work on.

19
00:01:02,646 --> 00:01:07,211
This lets you and the assistant refer to
the same function and the same tests.

20
00:01:09,546 --> 00:01:11,567
Ask two questions together.

21
00:01:11,650 --> 00:01:15,443
“Why does zero pass, and which baseline
test covers it?”

22
00:01:15,498 --> 00:01:20,069
The answer should show that greater than
or equal to zero returns true for zero.

23
00:01:20,725 --> 00:01:24,319
Read the tests for one, five, and
negative one.

24
00:01:24,430 --> 00:01:26,479
None runs the zero case.

25
00:01:26,534 --> 00:01:30,576
That gap explains why the existing suite
can be green while the issue remains

26
00:01:30,631 --> 00:01:31,118
open.

27
00:01:33,463 --> 00:01:37,450
Imagine an older attachment shows a
greater-than comparison.

28
00:01:37,505 --> 00:01:41,630
The active checkout still has greater
than or equal to zero.

29
00:01:41,713 --> 00:01:44,593
Open the current file and confirm the
branch,

30
00:01:44,648 --> 00:01:46,585
then remove the stale attachment.

31
00:01:47,279 --> 00:01:50,491
Ask the question again using the current
source.

32
00:01:50,574 --> 00:01:53,924
A one-character difference is enough to
change the answer,

33
00:01:53,979 --> 00:01:56,574
so keep the two versions easy to
distinguish.

34
00:01:58,852 --> 00:02:03,060
Try a follow-up where you provide the
function but leave out the issue.

35
00:02:03,144 --> 00:02:07,103
Ask what the program does and what
evidence would tell you whether that is

36
00:02:07,158 --> 00:02:07,796
correct.

37
00:02:08,487 --> 00:02:11,006
The code answers the first question.

38
00:02:11,062 --> 00:02:13,360
The issue answers the second.

39
00:02:13,443 --> 00:02:17,590
After you provide it, ask the assistant
to compare the two and check that it

40
00:02:17,645 --> 00:02:19,982
keeps both claims tied to their sources.

41
00:02:22,338 --> 00:02:26,408
Run the same question in two fresh
sessions on the same branch.

42
00:02:26,491 --> 00:02:28,955
In the first attach the issue alone.

43
00:02:29,038 --> 00:02:31,608
In the second add the validator and
tests.

44
00:02:32,298 --> 00:02:35,344
Compare their claims about the cause and
coverage.

45
00:02:35,427 --> 00:02:38,638
Look for a real source line and a named
test.

46
00:02:38,721 --> 00:02:42,377
Write one sentence about which
attachment made each claim checkable.
